<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sobhuza II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1982">1982</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sobhuza II was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Swaziland</a> from 1899, ruling in person from 1921 until his death in 1982, one of the longest recorded reigns. He campaigned for the return of land lost to concessions under British rule and led the country to independence in 1968. In 1973 he suspended the constitution and banned political parties, ruling by decree.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sobhuza was born on 22 July 1899 at the Zombodze Royal Residence, the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngwane-v">Bhunu</a> (Ngwane V) and Inkhosikati Lomawa Ndwandwe. His father died suddenly while dancing the incwala when the boy was four months old, and Sobhuza was chosen king soon afterwards. His grandmother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labotsibeni-mdluli">Labotsibeni Mdluli</a> served as regent, with his great-uncle Prince Malunge helping to lead the nation. He studied at Lovedale in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-cape">Eastern Cape</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> before taking power on 22 December 1921, at the age of twenty-two.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Early in his reign Sobhuza tried to recover land occupied by white settlers in 1907. He led a delegation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to petition King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> and in 1929 took the question to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judicial-committee-of-the-privy-council">Judicial Committee of the Privy Council</a>, but lost under the Foreign Jurisdictions Act 1890, which placed the acts of British administrations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a> beyond the reach of British courts. In the colonial period his role was largely ceremonial, though he kept great influence as traditional head of the nation.</p><p>In the early 1960s he helped to bring about independence. He opposed the constitution proposed by the British, which would have made him a constitutional monarch, and through his advisory council formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imbokodvo-national-movement">Imbokodvo National Movement</a>, which won all the seats in the 1967 elections. Independence came on 6 September 1968. On 12 April 1973 he repealed the constitution and dissolved parliament, ruling as an absolute monarch. A constitution of 1978 provided for an electoral college of eighty members chosen by forty local councils known as tinkhundla.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sobhuza marked his Diamond Jubilee in 1981. He died on 21 August 1982 at Embo State House, aged 83. His reign of 82 years and 254 days is the longest precisely dated of any monarch on record. He kept many consorts, according to the Swaziland National Trust Commission 70 wives, who bore him 210 children, and was known as the Bull of the Swazi. A regent ruled after his death, and his young son Prince Makhosetive was crowned Mswati III in 1986. Much of the land and mineral wealth once owned by non-Swazi interests had come under Swazi control during his reign.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sobhuza II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Sobhuza II</b> reigned from 1899, when he was a few months old, until 1982, one of the longest reigns of any monarch in recorded history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swazi-kingdom">Swazi Kingdom · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1973 <b>Sobhuza II</b> of Swaziland suspended the constitution and ruled as an absolute monarch, and Nepal’s kings swung between direct and constitutional rule until the monarchy was abolished in 2008.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>The queen regent Labotsibeni led opposition until <b>Sobhuza II</b> came of age in 1921; he petitioned the Privy Council and later bought back land through a national fund.</blockquote><a href="/state/swaziland-protectorate">Swaziland Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zombodze. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbabane">Mbabane</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/eswatini">Eswatini</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-27.23,31.34&amp;year=1952" aria-label="Zombodze on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5374 3162 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sobhuza II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5824 3432h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5824 3432h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-27.23,31.34&amp;year=1952">What was here in 1952? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
